= undefined) {\n        if (deepEqual(oursContent, theirsContent)) {\n          merged[id] = oursContent;\n        } else {\n          conflicts.push({\n            documentId: id, path: '', base: undefined, ours: oursContent, theirs: theirsContent,\n          });\n          merged[id] = oursContent; // ours wins for display\n        }\n      } else if (oursContent !== undefined) {\n        merged[id] = oursContent;\n      } else if (theirsContent !== undefined) {\n        merged[id] = theirsContent;\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  \n  return { merged, conflicts, applied: conflicts.length === 0 };\n}\n```\n\nOK wait, there's an issue with the \"both modified\" handling. If both modified differently, I shouldn't skip straight to field-level merge. What if one side changed a field and the other changed a completely different field? The field-level merge handles that.\n\nBut there's also the case where both modified, but the changes are compatible (no field overlaps). In that case, we should auto-merge. My recursive function handles that correctly - it returns conflicts only for paths that actually conflict.\n\nSo actually, I should always do field-level merge when both sides changed. Let me reconsider.\n\nThe basic flow is:\n1. If neither changed: skip\n2. If only ours changed: take ours\n3. If only theirs changed: take theirs\n4. If both changed but to the same value: take either\n5. If both changed differently: field-level merge (recursive three-way)\n\nCase 5 always goes through the recursive merge, which produces conflicts for conflicting paths and takes ours for those paths in the merged result. Non-conflicting paths get merged correctly.\n\nSo the \"both modified differently\" case should always go to field-level merge. Let me simplify:\n\n```typescript\n// Both modified (or one deleted and other modified - already handled)\nif (!deepEqual(oursContent, baseContent) && !deepEqual(theirsContent, baseContent)) {\n  // Both changed\n  if (deepEqual(oursContent, theirsConten