d\n      merged[key] = ours[key];\n    } else if (!inBase && inTheirs) {\n      // Only theirs added\n      merged[key] = theirs[key];\n    }\n    // key not in any of them → shouldn't happen since we union the keys\n  }\n  \n  // Handle case where ours or theirs is an empty object but base has keys (all removed)\n  // The Set union above handles this: base keys not in ours → the `inBase && !inOurs && inTheirs` case\n  // Similarly for base keys not in theirs\n  \n  // Hmm, but what if a key is in base but not in ours AND not in theirs?\n  // Both removed the same key → no conflict, don't include in merged\n  // Our current logic doesn't handle this case. Let me fix.\n  \n  // Actually, the Set union only includes keys present in at least one.\n  // If a key is in base, it's in the set. If it's not in ours AND not in theirs,\n  // we skip it (both removed), which is correct.\n  // But wait, that's not handled by any case. Let me add it.\n  \n  // I need to also handle: inBase && !inOurs && !inTheirs\n  // Both removed the key → don't include (correct for merge)\n  // This is a no-op skip, which is what my current code does implicitly.\n  \n  // Actually, let me double check the cases:\n  // For key in base:\n  //   if inOurs && inTheirs: merge\n  //   if inOurs && !inTheirs: theirs removed\n  //   if !inOurs && inTheirs: ours removed\n  //   if !inOurs && !inTheirs: both removed → skip ✓ (implicitly handled)\n  \n  // For key not in base:\n  //   if inOurs && inTheirs: both added\n  //   if inOurs && !inTheirs: only ours added ✓\n  //   if !inOurs && inTheirs: only theirs added ✓\n  //   if !inOurs && !inTheirs: not in any → can't happen\n  \n  // OK, the logic handles all cases implicitly. \n  \n  // But wait, there's a case I'm not handling: what if a key is in base but \n  // is undefined in ours/theirs? JavaScript `key in obj` returns false for\n  // undefined values that use JSON notation (since JSON.stringify drops undefined keys).\n  // So this is fine - we treat missing keys as absent.\n\n  return { merged, conflicts: allConflicts };\n}\n\nfunction mergeLeaf(\n  base: any, ours: any, theirs: any, docId: string, path: string\n): { merged: any, conflicts: Conflict[] } {\n  if (deepEqual(ours, theirs)) {\n    return { merged: ours, conflicts: [] };\n  }\n  if (deepEqual(base, ours)) {\n    return { merged: theirs, conflicts: [] };\n  }\n  if (deepEqual(base, theirs)) {\n    return { merged: ours, conflicts: [] };\n  }\n  return {\n    merged: ours, // ours wins for display\n    conflicts: [{ documentId: docId, path, base, ours, theirs }],\n  };\n}\n```\n\nOK this is getting quite thorough. Let me now write the actual code files.\n\nOne more thing: for the document-level case in the store's merge function, when `baseContent` is undefined (doc didn't exist at fork time), I should handle the case differently. Here's the flow:\n\n```\nif (baseContent !== undefined) {\n  // Doc existed at fork time\n  if (oursDeleted && theirsDeleted) continue; // both deleted\n  if (oursDeleted && !theirsDeleted) → conflict (root)\n  if (!oursDeleted && theirsDeleted) → conflict (root)\n  // Both still exist → field-level merge\n} else {\n  // Doc didn't exist at fork time\n  if (oursExists && theirsExists && same) → no conflict, take it\n  if (oursExists && theirsExists && different) → conflict (root)\n  if (oursExists && !theirsExists) → no conflict, take ours\n  if (!oursExists && theirsExists) → no conflict, take theirs\n  if (!oursExists && !theirsExists) → skip\n}\n```\n\nAlright, I'm going to write the full implementation now. Let me structure the code.\n\nOne last thought on `deepEqual`: I'll use JSON.stringify comparison for simplicity. This works for plain objects, arrays, primitives. It has the limitation of being order-sensitive for object keys, but since JSON.stringify on V8 serializes keys in insertion order, and our deepClone uses JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(...)), the order should be consistent. Actually, the safer bet is to implement a proper deep equality check:\n\n```typescript\nfunction deepEqual(a: any, b: any): boolean {\n  if (a === b) return true;\n  if (typeof a !== typeof b) return false;\n  if (a === null || b === null) return a === b;\n  if (typeof a !== 'object') return a === b;\n  if (Array.isArray(a) !== Array.isArray(b)) return false;\n  if (Array.isArray(a)) {\n    if (a.length !== b.length) return false;\n    return a.every((v, i) => deepEqual(v, b[i]));\n  }\n  const keysA = Object.keys(a);\n  const keysB = Object.keys(b);\n  if (keysA.length !== keysB.length) return false;\n  return keysA.every(k => k in b && deepEqual(a[k], b[k]));\n}\n```\n\nThis is more reliable. Let me use this.\n\nNow let me also think about one more edge case in merge: what if a doc was created after the fork on one branch, and the other branch also independently created a doc with the same ID but different content? That's a conflict at the document level. My logic handles this in the `else` branch (baseContent undefined).\n\nAnd what about the case where a doc exists in base and is deleted on one branch but the other branch also changes it? That's a conflict at the root path. I'll represent that as `path: ''`.\n\nOne more thing: when the merge succeeds (no conflicts), I need to update the current branch's documents. For each document in `merged`, I update the current branch's doc and create a new version. I should also handle the case where merged has fewer docs than current (some were deleted in the merge).\n\nActually, re-reading the spec: \"A merge with no changes on either side is a no-op (fast-forward).\" So if neither branch changed anything, nothing happens. But also, if the merge has no conflicts and no actual changes (empty merged), it's a no-op.\n\nLet me code it now. I'll write three files:\n\n1. `src/types.ts` - Type definitions\n2. `src/merger.ts` - Merge logic (threeWayMergeObject, deepEqual, isPlainObject)\n3. `src/store.ts` - DocumentStore implementation  \n4. `src/index.ts` - Re-