ork time)\\n  snapshot: Map<string, object>; \\n}\\n```\\n\\nWhen creating a branch:\\n```typescript\\nconst snapshot = new Map<string, object>();\\nfor (const [id, entry] of sourceDocs) {\\n  if (!entry.deleted) {\\n    snapshot.set(id, deepClone(entry.content));\\n  }\\n}\\nthis.branchForks.set(name, { fromBranch: sourceBranchName, snapshot });\\n```\\n\\nNow, in the merge, I get the base from the fork info:\\n```typescript\\n// Build base state from snapshot\\nconst baseState = new Map<string, object>();\\nif (baseFork) {\\n  for (const [id, content] of baseFork.snapshot) {\\n    baseState.set(id, content);\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nIf there's no fork info, baseState is empty.\\n\\nNow, in the merge function, I iterate over all document IDs from baseState, currentDocs, and sourceDocs. For each:\\n\\n```typescript\\nconst baseContent = baseState.get(id);\\nconst currentEntry = currentDocs.get(id);\\nconst sourceEntry = sourceDocs.get(id);\\n\\nconst currentContent = currentEntry && !currentEntry.deleted ? currentEntry.content : undefined;\\nconst sourceContent = sourceEntry && !sourceEntry.deleted ? sourceEntry.content : undefined;\\n```\\n\\nIf baseContent !== undefined:\\n  - if currentContent === undefined && sourceContent === undefined: skip (both deleted)\\n  - if currentContent === undefined: conflict (ours deleted, theirs modified)\\n  - if sourceContent === undefined: conflict (theirs deleted, ours modified)\\n  - if deepEqual(currentContent, baseContent) && deepEqual(sourceContent, baseContent): skip (neither changed)\\n  - if deepEqual(currentContent, baseContent): only source changed → take theirs\\n  - if deepEqual(sourceContent, baseContent): only current changed → take ours\\n  - if deepEqual(currentContent, sourceContent): both same → take either\\n  - else: field-level merge\\n\\nIf baseContent === undefined:\\n  - if currentContent && sourceContent && deepEqual(currentContent, sourceContent): take it\\n  - if currentContent && sourceContent && !deepEqual(currentContent, sourceContent): conflict (root)\\n  - if currentContent: take ours\\n  - if sourceContent: take theirs\\n  - else: skip\\n\\nOK, I think I have a solid plan. Let me write the code.\\n\\nOh wait, one more thing. For the `merged` field in `MergeResult`, the spec says it's `Record<string, object>` - documentId to merged content. This should only include documents that changed or were created by the merge.\\n\\nShould I include documents that didn't change? I'll say no - I'll only include docs that appear in the merged set. This means for a fast-forward merge with no changes on either side, `merged` would be empty.\\n\\nActually, should I always include all documents in the merged result? \\\"The `merged` field always contains the merge result (with conflicts resolved in favor of 'ours' for the purpose of showing what it would look like).\\\" This suggests it should contain all documents that were part of the merge, not just changed ones. Hmm, but for documents that didn't change on either side, including them would just be noise.\\n\\nI'll include only documents that were affected by the merge (at least one side changed something). For a fast-forward with no changes, merged would be empty.\\n\\nLet me now write the code. I'll be thorough.\\n\\nActually, let me reconsider one thing. In the merge, when I say \\\"field-level merge,\\\" I'm doing a recursive three-way comparison of the object trees. The result is a merged object that combines non-conflicting changes from both sides. For conflicting paths, the merged value uses \\\"ours.\\\"\\n\\nThis merged object should be the full document content, not just the changed parts. So `merged[id]` is the complete document after applying the merge.\\n\\nOK, I'm ready to code. Let me be careful and thorough.\\n\\nLet me also consider: should `deepEqual` compare arrays element-by-element? Yes.\\n\\n```typescript\\nfunction deepEqual(a: unknown, b: unknown): boolean {\\n  if (a === b) return true;\\n  if (a === 