nt\":[{\"type\":\"thinking\",\"thinking\":\"The user wants me to build a versioned document store with structural merge capabilities. Let me analyze the requirements carefully and implement this step by step.\\n\\nLet me plan out the implementation:\\n\\n1. **Document Store** - In-memory store with CRUD operations\\n2. **Version History** - Every create/update creates a version entry\\n3. **Branching** - Branch management with independent state per branch\\n4. **Three-way Merge** - Merge branches with structural comparison\\n\\nI'll structure the implementation across a few files:\\n- `src/index.ts` - Main entry point exporting `DocumentStore`\\n- `src/store.ts` - The core `DocumentStore` class\\n- `src/merger.ts` - Three-way merge logic\\n- `src/types.ts` - Type definitions\\n\\nLet me think through the data model:\\n\\n```typescript\\ninterface BranchState {\\n  documents: Map<string, DocumentState>;\\n}\\n\\ninterface DocumentState {\\n  content: object;\\n  versions: Version[];\\n  deleted: boolean;\\n  // For tracking what was at each version for merge purposes\\n}\\n```\\n\\nFor branching:\\n- Each branch has its own document map\\n- When branching, deep clone the current branch's documents\\n- When switching branches, change the active branch pointer\\n\\nFor merging:\\n- Three-way merge: find LCA (lowest common ancestor) - the branch point\\n- Compare base, ours (current branch), theirs (source branch)\\n- Per-document, per-field comparison\\n\\nKey challenge: tracking the branch point / common ancestor. When a branch is created from another branch, that's the divergence point. We need to snapshot the state at that point.\\n\\nLet me think about the merge more carefully:\\n\\nThe \\\"common ancestor state\\\" is the state of the branch when `createBranch` was called. So when we create branch `feature` from `main`, we snapshot `main`'s state at that moment. Later when merging `feature` back into `main`, we use that snapshot as the base.\\n\\nWait, but what if