elds. The spec says documents are plain JSON objects but fields may contain arrays. For the diff, arrays should be treated as leaf values (we don't try to deep-merge arrays). So `a.b` where `b` is an array: if both branches changed the array differently, that's a conflict at path \\\"a.b\\\".\\n\\nOK, let me now implement this.\\n\\nData structures:\\n\\n```typescript\\n// Internal\\ninterface DocumentEntry {\\n  content: object;\\n  versions: Version[];\\n  deleted: boolean;\\n}\\n\\ninterface BranchData {\\n  documents: Map<string, DocumentEntry>;\\n}\\n\\nclass DocumentStoreImpl {\\n  private branches: Map<string, BranchData>;\\n  private currentBranch: string;\\n  private snapshots: Map<string, Map<string, BranchData>>; // branch -> branchAtCreation -> snapshot\\n  \\n  // Actually, simpler: each branch just tracks its state.\\n  // For merge, we need to know the base state. \\n  // When branch B is created from branch A, we snapshot A's state.\\n  // Store: branchCreateSnapshots: Map<string, {branch: string, state: BranchData}>\\n}\\n```\\n\\nHmm, actually let me think about this differently. When `createBranch('feature', 'main')` is called:\\n1. We snapshot `main`'s current state\\n2. We create a new branch `feature` with that snapshot\\n\\nLater when merging `feature` into `currentBranch`, we need the base (common ancestor) state. That's the snapshot taken when the branch was created from the other branch.\\n\\nBut branches can be created from other branches, and merges can happen. The \\\"base\\\" for a merge is the state at which the branches diverged. For simplicity, I'll track the base state at branch creation time.\\n\\nActually wait - what if main has N branches, and feature was branched from main at some point, then later someone creates a branch from feature? The common ancestor between main and feature is still the snapshot taken when feature was created from main.\\n\\nLet me keep it simpler: each branch stores its creation metadata including what branch it was created from and the state at that point.\\n\\n```\\ninterface BranchInfo {\\n  name: string;\\n  createdAtBranch: string | null;  // which branch we branched from\\n  baseState: Map<string, DocumentEntry> | null;  // snapshot at creation\\n  documents: Map<string, DocumentEntry>;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nWait, this over-complicates things. Let me just store a version history per branch-document pair and use that for merge.\\n\\nActually, simplest approach:\\n\\n```typescript\\ninterface BranchState {\\n  documents: Map<string, DocumentEntry>;  // current state\\n}\\n\\n// For merge, we need base. We'll store branch-creation snapshots.\\nconst branchForks: Map<string, {from: string, snapshot: Map<string, DocumentEntry>}>;\\n```\\n\\nWhen `createBranch('feature', 'main')` is called:\\n1. Snapshot main's current documents\\n2. Create `feature` branch with deep clone of main's documents\\n3. Record that `feature` forked from `main` with snapshot S\\n\\nWhen `merge('feature')` is called on `main`:\\n1. Find `feature`'s fork record → base is the snapshot\\n2. Three-way merge: base vs main (ours) vs feature (theirs)\\n\\nBut wait, what if `feature` was already merged once, and then more changes happen? The base shouldn't change. The common ancestor is still the fork point. That's fine.\\n\\nBut what about more complex scenarios? Say:\\n1. Branch main at state M0\\n2. Create feature from main → fork snapshot F0 = M0\\n3. Modify main → M1\\n4. Modify feature → F1\\n5. Merge feature into main → three-way merge M0 vs M1 vs F1\\n6. Continue working on both branches\\n\\nThe base for the NEXT merge should still be M0 (original fork point). Actually, that depends on the merge semantics. If feature was merged into main, the new common ancestor for subsequent merges would be... hmm.\\n\\nFor simplicity and correctness in a basic implementation, I'll track the fork point as the base. Subsequent merges use the same base. This is how Git works with merge commits, but since we don't have merge commits in this model, the fork point (merge base) remains the same unless the user creates a new branch.\\n\\nActually, let me keep it really simple: the base state is always the snapshot taken at branch creation time. This handles the common case well and keeps the implementation straightforward.\\n\\nLet me now code this up.\\n\\n```typescript\\n// types.ts\\nexport interface Version {\\n  id: string;\\n  timestamp: number;\\n  content: object;\\n}\\n\\nexport interface MergeResult {\\n  merged: Record<string, object>;\\n  conflicts: Conflict[];\\n  applied: boolean;\\n}\\n\\nexport interface Conflict {\\n  documentId: string;\\n  path: string;\\n  base: any;\\n  ours: any;\\n  theirs: any;\\n}\\n\\nexport interface DocumentStore {\\n  create(id: string, content: object): void;\\n  get(id: string): object;\\n  update(id: string, content: object): void;\\n  delete(id: string): void;\\n  getHistory(id: string): Version[];\\n  getVersion(id: string, versionId: string): object;\\n  createBranch(name: string, fromBranch?: string): void;\\n  switchBranch(name: string): void;\\n  getBranch(): string;\\n  listBranches(): string[];\\n  merge(sourceBranch: string): MergeResult;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nNow the store:\\n\\n```typescript\\n// store.ts\\nimport { Version, MergeResult, Conflict, DocumentStore } from './types';\\nimport { threeWayMerge } from './merger';\\n\\ninterface DocumentEntry {\\n  content: object;\\n  versions: Version[];\\n  deleted: boolean;  // soft delete to track in version history\\n}\\n\\ninterface BranchSnapshot {\\n  branchName: string;  // the branch this was forked from\\n  documents: Map<string, { content: object }>;  // snapshot at fork time\\n}\\n\\nclass DocumentStoreImpl implements DocumentStore {\\n  private branches: Map<string, Map<string, DocumentEntry>> = new Map();\\n  private currentBranchName: string = 'main';\\n  private branchForks: Map<string, BranchSnapshot> = new Map();\\n  private versionCounter = 0;\\n\\n  constructor() {\\n    this.branches.set('main', new Map());\\n  }\\n\\n  private getCurrentBranchDocs(): Map<string, DocumentEntry> {\\n    const docs = this.branches.get(this.currentBranchName);\\n    if (!docs) throw new Error(`Branch \\\"${this.currentBranchName}\\\" not found`);\\n    return docs;\\n  }\\n\\n  private getDoc(id: string): DocumentEntry {\\n    const docs = this.getCurrentBranchDocs();\\n    const doc = docs.get(id);\\n    if (!doc) throw new Error(`Document \\\"${id}\\\" not found`);\\n    if (doc.deleted) throw new Error(`Document \\\"${id}\\\" has been deleted`);\\n    return doc;\\n  }\\n\\n  private createVersion(content: object): Version {\\n    this.versionCounter++;\\n    return {\\n      id: `v${this.versionCounter}`,\\n      timestamp: Date.now(),\\n      content: JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(content)),\\n    };\\n  }\\n\\n  private deepClone<T>(obj: T): T {\\n    return JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(obj));\\n  }\\n\\n  create(id: string, content: object): void {\\n    const docs = this.getCurrentBranchDocs();\\n    if (docs.has(id) && !docs.get(id)!.deleted) {\\n      throw new Error(`Document \\\"${id}\\\" already exists`);\\n    }\\n    const version = this.createVersion(content);\\n    const entry: DocumentEntry = {\\n      content: this.deepClone(content),\\n      versions: [version],\\n      deleted: false,\\n    };\\n    docs.set(id, entry);\\n  }\\n\\n  get(id: string): object {\\n    return this.deepClone(this.getDoc(id).content);\\n  }\\n\\n  update(id: string, content: object): void {\\n    const entry = this.getDoc(id);\\n    entry.content = this.deepClone(content);\\n    const version = this.createVersion(content);\\n    entry.versions.push(version);\\n  }\\n\\n  delete(id: string): void {\\n    const entry = this.getDoc(id);\\n    entry.deleted = true;\\n    // Optionally create a version marking the deletion? \\n    // The spec doesn't mention creating versions for deletes. Let me re-read...\\n    // \\\"Every create and update generates a new version\\\" - delete does not create a version.\\n  }\\n\\n  getHistory(id: string): Version[] {\\n    // getHistory can return history even for deleted documents\\n    const docs = this.getCurrentBranchDocs();\\n    const doc = docs.get(id);\\n    if (!doc) throw new Error(`Document \\\"${id}\\\" not found`);\\n    // If the doc was created and then deleted, we still return history\\n    return this.deepClone(doc.versions);\\n  }\\n\\n  getVersion(id: string, versionId: string): object {\\n    const docs = this.getCurrentBranchDocs();\\n    const doc = docs.get(id);\\n    if (!doc) throw new Error(`Document \\\"${id}\\\" not found`);\\n    const version = doc.versions.find(v => v.id === versionId);\\n    if (!version) throw new Error(`Version \\\"${versionId}\\\" not found for document \\\"${id}\\\"`);\\n    return this.deepClone(version.content);\\n  }\\n\\n  createBranch(name: string, fromBranch?: string): void {\\n    if (this.branches.has(name)) {\\n      throw new Error(`Branch \\\"${name}\\\" already exists`);\\n    }\\n\\n    const sourceBranch = fromBranch || this.currentBranchName;\\n    const sourceDocs = this.branches.get(sourceBranch);\\n    if (!sourceDocs) {\\n      throw new Error(`Source branch \\\"${sourceBranch}\\\" does not exist`);\\n    }\\n\\n    // Deep clone the documents from the source branch\\n    const newDocs = new Map<string, DocumentEntry>();\\n    for (const [id, entry] of sourceDocs) {\\n      newDocs.set(id, this.deepClone(entry));\\n    }\\n\\n    this.branches.set(name, newDocs);\\n\\n    // Record the fork snapshot for merge base calculation\\n    const snapshot = new Map<string, { content: object }>();\\n    for (const [id, entry] of sourceDocs) {\\n      snapshot.set(id, { content: this.deepClone(entry.content) });\\n    }\\n    this.branchForks.set(name, {\\n      branchName: sourceBranch,\\n      documents: snapshot,\\n    });\\n  }\\n\\n  switchBranch(name: string): void {\\n    if (!this.branches.has(name)) {\\n      throw new Error(`Branch \\\"${name}\\\" does not exist`);\\n    }\\n    this.currentBranchName = name;\\n  }\\n\\n  getBranch(): string {\\n    return this.currentBranchName;\\n  }\\n\\n  listBranches(): string[] {\\n    return Array.from(this.branches.keys());\\n  }\\n\\n  merge(sourceBranch: string): MergeResult {\\n    const sourceDocs = this.branches.get(sourceBranch);\\n    if (!sourceDocs) {\\n      throw new Error(`Source branch \\\"${sourceBranch}\\\" does not exist`);\\n    }\\n\\n    const currentDocs = this.getCurrentBranchDocs();\\n    const forkInfo = this.branchForks.get(sourceBranch);\\n\\n    // Compute base state\\n    const baseDocs: Map<string, DocumentEntry> = forkInfo\\n      ? new Map(Object.entries(forkInfo.documents).map(([id, { content }]) => [\\n          id,\\n          { content: this.deepClone(content), versions: [], deleted: false }\\n        ]))\\n      : new Map();\\n\\n    // Wait, I need to fix the fork snapshot storage. Let me restructure.\\n    // forkInfo.documents is currently a Map<string, {content: object}>\\n    // I need to convert it properly.\\n\\n    // Actually let me reconsider the data structure for baseDocs.\\n\\n    // For all unique document IDs across base, ours, theirs:\\n    const allDocIds = new Set<string>();\\n    for (const id of baseDocs.keys()) allDocIds.add(id);\\n    for (const id of sourceDocs.keys()) allDocIds.add(id);\\n    for (const id of currentDocs.keys()) allDocIds.add(id);\\n\\n    // Hmm, this has issues since my forkInfo.documents is structured differently.\\n    // Let me redo the branching data structure.\\n\\n    // I'll change branchForks to store a plain object map: Record<string, {content: object}>\\n    // Actually, let me change to store it more cleanly.\\n\\n    // Let me update the implementation...\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nOK, I'm running into complexity with the data structures. Let me clean it up in the actual implementation.\\n\\nLet me plan the file structure:\\n- `src/index.ts` - exports the DocumentStore class\\n- `src/types.ts` - type definitions\\n- `src/merger.ts` - three-way merge logic\\n\\nLet me now write the actual implementation carefully.\\n\\nFor the fork tracking, I'll store a simple record: `Map<string, { fromBranch: string, baseDocs: Record<string, object> }>` where `baseDocs` maps document IDs to their content at the time of branching.\\n\\nFor the merge, I need to handle:\\n1. Documents that exist in base but were deleted on one or both branches\\n2. Documents created on one branch (don't exist in base)\\n3. Documents modified on one or both branches\\n\\nLet me think about the structural comparison. For each document that exists in any of the three states:\\n\\nIf a doc exists in base:\\n- Both modified: compare field by field\\n- Only ours modified: accept ours\\n- Only theirs modified: accept theirs\\n- Neither modified: skip\\n- One deleted, other modified: conflict at root path \\\"\\\"\\n- One deleted, other also deleted: skip\\n\\nIf a doc doesn't exist in base (created on a branch):\\n- Exists on both but different content: conflict (document-level)\\n- Exists on only one: take it (no conflict)\\n\\nFor field-level comparison, I need to do a recursive diff between the objects and identify which paths changed.\\n\\n```typescript\\nfunction diff(base: object, target: object): Set<string> {\\n  // Returns set of dot-paths that differ between base and target\\n  // For objects, recurse; for primitives/arrays, compare directly\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThen for each leaf path that changed on either side:\\n- Changed on only one: accept that side's value\\n- Changed on both with same value: no conflict\\n- Changed on both with different value: conflict\\n\\nLet me implement `diff` properly:\\n\\n```typescript\\nfunction getChangedPaths(base: any, target: any, prefix: string = ''): Map<string, {base: any, value: any}> {\\n  const changed = new Map<string, {base: any, value: any}>();\\n  \\n  if (base === target) return changed; // same reference (shouldn't happen with deep clones but safe)\\n  \\n  if (typeof base !== 'object' || base === null || typeof target !== 'object' || target === null) {\\n    // One or both are primitives (or arrays - treat as leaf values)\\n    if (!simpleEqual(base, target)) {\\n      changed.set(prefix, { base, value: target });\\n    }\\n    return changed;\\n  }\\n  \\n  if (Array.isArray(base) || Array.isArray(target)) {\\n    // Arrays are leaf values\\n    if (!arrayEqual(base, target)) {\\n      changed.set(prefix, { base, value: target });\\n    }\\n    return changed;\\n  }\\n  \\n  // Both are plain objects\\n  const allKeys = new Set([...Object.keys(base), ...Object.keys(target)]);\\n  for (const key of allKeys) {\\n    const childPrefix = prefix ? `${prefix}.${key}` : key;\\n    \\n    if (key in base && key in target) {\\n      // Both have the key - recurse\\n      const subChanges = getChangedPaths(base[key], target[key], childPrefix);\\n      for (const [path, change] of subChanges) {\\n        changed.set(path, change);\\n      }\\n    } else if (key in base) {\\n      // Key was removed from target\\n      changed.set(childPrefix, { base: base[key], value: undefined });\\n    } else {\\n      // Key was added in target\\n      changed.set(childPrefix, { base: undefined, value: target[key] });\\n    }\\n  }\\n  \\n  return changed;\\n}\\n```\\n\\nWait, this approach has issues. When comparing a complex object, if `base.a` is `{b: 1, c: 2}` and `target.a` is `{b: 1, c: 3}`, I'd want the path `a.c` to show as changed, not `a`. And if `base.a` is `{b: 1}` and `target.a` is `[1, 2, 3]`, then `a` changed as a whole (since one is an object and the other is an array).\\n\\nBut the tricky part is handling the merge correctly. Let me simplify: the `merged` result should always produce a valid merged document. For each document, I'll compute the merged content by applying the non-conflicting changes and using \\\"ours\\\" for conflicting paths.\\n\\nActually, re-reading the spec:\\n- \\\"The `merged` field always contains the merge result (with conflicts resolved in favor of 'ours' for the purpose of showing what it would look like).\\\"\\n\\nSo even with conflicts, we compute the merged result but don't apply it.\\n\\nLet me structure the merge logic:\\n\\n```\\nFor each document in (base ∪ ours ∪ theirs):\\n  If doc in base:\\n    - Calculate ours paths: diff(base, oursContent)\\n    - Calculate theirs paths: diff(base, theirsContent)\\n    - For each unique path:\\n      - Only ours: take ours\\n      - Only theirs: take theirs\\n      - Both same value: no conflict\\n      - Both different: conflict (record)\\n    - Also check: if doc deleted on one side but modified on other → conflict\\n    \\n  If doc NOT in base (created on one side):\\n    - If exists on both branches but with different content → conflict\\n    - If exists on only one → take it\\n```\\n\\nHmm, for the \\\"deleted on one side, modified on other\\\" case, I think the simplest approach is to treat deletion as a change to the entire document. So if base + ours path shows the doc as deleted (no content), and theirs shows changes... that's a conflict.\\n\\nActually, let me reconsider. For the `diff` approach, if a document was deleted on ours, I should represent that as: all paths were set to \\\"deleted\\\" (or rather, the document doesn't exist). But that doesn't map well to path-level diff.\\n\\nLet me think about this differently. Instead of doing path-level diffs, let me first categorize each document:\\n\\n1. **Doc exists in base:**\\n   a. Both modified: do field-level merge\\n   b. Only ours modified (theirs same as base): take ours\\n   c. Only theirs modified (ours same as base): take theirs\\n   d. Neither modified: skip\\n   e. Ours deleted, theirs modified: conflict (root path)\\n   f. Ours modified, theirs deleted: conflict (root path)\\n   g. Both deleted: skip\\n\\n2. **Doc doesn't exist in base:**\\n   a. Created on ours only: take ours\\n   b. Created on theirs only: take theirs\\n   c. Created on both with same content: no conflict\\n   d. Created on both with different content: conflict (root path)\\n\\nFor case 1a, I need the field-level diff. Let me implement a `computeThreeWayMerge` function that takes base, ours, theirs and returns `{merged: object, conflicts: Conflict[]}`.\\n\\nHere's my approach for the field-level merge:\\n\\n```typescript\\nfunction threeWayMergeObject(\\n  base: any, ours: any, theirs: any,\\n  docId: string, prefix: string = ''\\n): { merged: any, conflicts: Conflict[] } {\\n  // Handle non-object cases\\n  if (typeof base !== 'object' || base === null || Array.isArray(base) ||\\n      typeof ours !== 'object' || ours === null || Array.isArray(ours) ||\\n      typeof theirs !== 'object' || theirs === null || Array.isArray(theirs)) {\\n    // Leaf values - compare directly\\n    if (deepEqual(ours, theirs)) {\\n      return { merged: ours, conflicts: [] };\\n    }\\n    if (deepEqual(base, ours)) {\\n      return { merged: theirs, conflicts: [] };\\n    }\\n    if (deepEqual(base, theirs)) {\\n      return { merged: ours, conflicts: [] };\\n    }\\n    // Both changed differently\\n    return {\\n      merged: ours, // ours wins for display\\n      conflicts: [{\\n        documentId: docId,\\n        path: prefix,\\n        base,\\n        ours,\\n        theirs,\\n      }],\\n    };\\n  }\\n  \\n  // All three are objects\\n  const allKeys = new Set([...Object.keys(base), ...Object.keys(ours), ...Object.keys(theirs)]);\\n  const merged: any = {};\\n  const allConflicts: Conflict[] = [];\\n  \\n  for (const key of allKeys) {\\n    const path = prefix ? `${prefix}.${key}` : key;\\n    const hasBase = key in base;\\n    const hasOurs = key in ours;\\n    const hasTheirs = key in theirs;\\n    \\n    if (hasBase && hasOurs && hasTheirs) {\\n      const result = threeWayMergeObject(base[key], ours[key], theirs[key], docId, path);\\n      if (result.merged !== undefined) {\\n        merged[key] = result.merged;\\n      }\\n      allConflicts.push(...result.conflicts);\\n    } else if (hasBase && hasOurs && !hasTheirs) {\\n      // Theirs removed this key\\n      if (deepEqual(base[key], ours[key])) {\\n        // Ours didn't change it either → accept removal\\n        // Don't add to merged (key removed)\\n      } else {\\n        // Ours changed it → conflict\\n        allConflicts.push({\\n          documentId: docId,\\n          path,\\n          base: base[key],\\n          ours: ours[key],\\n          theirs: undefined,\\n        });\\n        merged[key] = ours[key]; // ours wins for display\\n      }\\n    } else if (hasBase && !hasOurs && hasTheirs) {\\n      // Ours removed this key\\n      if (deepEqual(base[key], theirs[key])) {\\n        // Theirs didn't change → accept removal\\n        // Don't add to merged\\n      } else {\\n        // Theirs changed it → conflict\\n        allConflicts.push({\\n          documentId: docId,\\n          path,\\n          base: base[key],\\n          ours: undefined,\\n          theirs: theirs[key],\\n        });\\n        // Ours is undefined, so for display we... hmm, what goes in merged?\\n        // Spec says \\\"conflicts resolved in favor of ours\\\"\\n        // So we keep it removed. But for display of \\\"what it would look like\\\",\\n        // I'll include it with theirs value. Actually no, \\\"in favor of ours\\\" means\\n        // ours wins, so it stays removed.\\n      }\\n    } else if (!hasBase && hasOurs && hasTheirs) {\\n      // Both added this key\\n      if (deepEqual(ours[key], theirs[key])) {\\n        merged[key] = ours[key];\\n      } else {\\n        allConflicts.push({\\n          documentId: docId,\\n          path,\\n          base: undefined,\\n          ours: ours[key],\\n          theirs: theirs[key],\\n        });\\n        merged[key] = ours[key]; // ours wins\\n      }\\n    } else if (!hasBase && hasOurs) {\\n      // Only ours added this key\\n      merged[key] = ours[key];\\n    } else if (!hasBase && hasTheirs) {\\n      // Only theirs added this key\\n      merged[key] = theirs[key];\\n    }\\n  }\\n  \\n  return { merged, conflicts: allConflicts };\\n}\\n```\\n\\nHmm, this is getting complex but manageable. Let me also handle the case where `base` exists as a primitive/null but `ours`/`theirs` is an object. Actually, my recursive function handles this: if any of the three aren't plain objects (or are arrays), we treat them as leaf values.\\n\\nWait, but I have a subtle issue. When all three are present but one is an object and another is a primitive, the function will recurse into the object and compare with the primitive... it won't handle that well. Let me fix the base case detection.\\n\\nActually, my first check is:\\n```js\\nif (typeof base !== 'object' || base === null || Array.isArray(base) ||\\n    typeof ours !== 'object' || ours === null || Array.isArray(ours) ||\\n    typeof theirs !== 'object' || theirs === null || Array.isArray(theirs)) {\\n```\\nThis catches the case where any of the three is not a plain object. So if `base` is a primitive but `ours` is an object, we go into the leaf value comparison path. Good.\\n\\nBut wait, that's not entirely right either. If `base = {a: 1}`, `ours = {a: 1, b: 2}`, `theirs = {a: 1}`, the recursion works fine. But if `base = 5`, `ours = {x: 1}`, `theirs = 5`, then we hit the leaf case where `ours !== theirs` and `base === theirs`, so we'd take ours without conflict. That seems right - one side changed a leaf to a complex object.\\n\\nOK I think the logic is sound. Let me now write the full implementation.\\n\\nFor the `deepEqual` helper, I'll use a simple comparison:\\n\\n```typescript\\nfunction deepEqual(a: any, b: any): boolean {\\n  return JSON.stringify(a) === JSON.stringify(b);\\n}\\n```\\n\\nOK, let me now plan the merge logic from the top level:\\n\\n```typescript\\nfunction computeMerge(\\n  baseDocs: Map<string, object>,\\n  currentDocs: Map<string, DocumentEntry>,\\n  sourceDocs: Map<string, DocumentEntry>\\n): MergeResult {\\n  const allIds = new Set<string>();\\n  for (const id of baseDocs.keys()) allIds.add(id);\\n  for (const [id, entry] of currentDocs) if (!entry.deleted) allIds.add(id);\\n  for (const [id, entry] of sourceDocs) if (!entry.deleted) allIds.add(id);\\n  \\n  const merged: Record<string, object> = {};\\n  const conflicts: Conflict[] = [];\\n  \\n  for (const id of allIds) {\\n    const base = baseDocs.get(id);\\n    const ours = currentDocs.get(id);\\n    const theirs = sourceDocs.get(id);\\n    \\n    const baseContent = base ? base.content : undefined;\\n    const oursContent = ours && !ours.deleted ? ours.content : undefined;\\n    const theirsContent = theirs && !theirs.deleted ? theirs.content : undefined;\\n    \\n    // If doc exists in base\\n    if (baseContent !== undefined) {\\n      // Both deleted → skip\\n      if (oursContent === undefined && theirsContent === undefined) continue;\\n      \\n      // One deleted, other modified → conflict\\n      if (oursContent === undefined && theirsContent !== undefined) {\\n        conflicts.push({\\n          documentId: id, path: '', base: baseContent, ours: undefined, theirs: theirsContent,\\n        });\\n        // merged shows ours=deleted (doc not included), but we show their version for display\\n        // Actually spec says \\\"resolved in favor of ours\\\" → deleted\\n        continue;\\n      }\\n      if (oursContent !== undefined && theirsContent === undefined) {\\n        conflicts.push({\\n          documentId: id, path: '', base: baseContent, ours: oursContent, theirs: undefined,\\n        });\\n        merged[id] = oursContent; // ours wins\\n        continue;\\n      }\\n      \\n      // Both modified\\n      if (deepEqual(oursContent, baseContent) && deepEqual(theirsContent, baseContent)) {\\n        // Neither changed\\n        continue;\\n      }\\n      if (deepEqual(oursContent, baseContent)) {\\n        // Only theirs changed\\n        merged[id] = theirsContent;\\n        continue;\\n      }\\n      if (deepEqual(theirsContent, baseContent)) {\\n        // Only ours changed\\n        merged[id] = oursContent;\\n        continue;\\n      }\\n      if (deepEqual(oursContent, theirsContent)) {\\n        // Both changed to the same value\\n        merged[id] = oursContent;\\n        continue;\\n      }\\n      \\n      // Both changed differently → field-level merge\\n      const result = threeWayMergeObject(baseContent, oursContent, theirsContent, id, '');\\n      merged[id] = result.merged;\\n      conflicts.push(...result.conflicts);\\n    } else {\\n      // Doc doesn't exist in base\\n      if (oursContent !== undefined && theirsContent !== undefined) {\\n        if (deepEqual(oursContent, theirsContent)) {\\n          merged[id] = oursContent;\\n        } else {\\n          conflicts.push({\\n            documentId: id, path: '', base: undefined, ours: oursContent, theirs: theirsContent,\\n          });\\n          merged[id] = oursContent; // ours wins for display\\n        }\\n      } else if (oursContent !== undefined) {\\n        merged[id] = oursContent;\\n      } else if (theirsContent !== undefined) {\\n        merged[id] = theirsContent;\\n      }\\n    }\\n  }\\n  \\n  return { merged, conflicts, applied: conflicts.length === 0 };\\n}\\n```\\n\\nOK wait, there's an issue with the \\\"both modified\\\" handling. If both modified differently, I shouldn't skip straight to field-level merge. What if one side changed a field and the other changed a completely different field? The field-level merge handles that.\\n\\nBut there's also the case where both modified, but the changes are compatible (no field overlaps). In that case, we should auto-merge. My recursive function handles that correctly - it returns conflicts only for paths that actually conflict.\\n\\nSo actually, I should always do field-level merge when both sides changed. Let me reconsider.\\n\\nThe basic flow is:\\n1. If neither changed: skip\\n2. If only ours changed: take ours\\n3. If only theirs changed: take theirs\\n4. If both changed but to the same value: take either\\n5. If both changed differently: field-level merge (recursive three-way)\\n\\nCase 5 always goes through the recursive merge, which produces conflicts for conflicting paths and takes ours for those paths in the merged result. Non-conflicting paths get merged correctly.\\n\\nSo the \\\"both modified differently\\\" case should always go to field-level merge. Let me simplify:\\n\\n```typescript\\n// Both modified (or one deleted and other modified - already handled)\\nif (!deepEqual(oursContent, baseContent) && !deepEqual(theirsContent, baseContent)) {\\n  // Both changed\\n  if (deepEqual(oursContent, theirsContent)) {\\n    // Same result\\n    merged[id] = oursContent;\\n  } else {\\n    // Different changes - field-level merge\\n    const result = threeWayMergeObject(baseContent, oursContent, theirsContent, id, '');\\n    merged[id] = result.merged;\\n    conflicts.push(...result.conflicts);\\n  }\\n}\\n```\\n\\nHmm actually wait, do I even need the `!deepEqual(oursContent, baseContent) && !deepEqual(theirsContent, baseContent)` check? If only one side changed, the field-level merge would just take that side's changes with no conflicts. So I could always run field-level merge for the case where both exist in base and both exist in ours/theirs (not deleted).\\n\\nActually no, the field-level merge is more expensive and might produce unnecessary path-level comparisons. Let me keep the optimized checks and only do field-level merge when both sides actually changed.\\n\\nBut actually, the field-level merge IS the correct way to do three-way merge. It handles all cases. If only one side changed, it takes that side's changes. If neither changed, it takes the base. If both changed to the same, no conflict. If both changed differently, conflict.\\n\\nSo I could simplify by always running field-level merge when the document exists on all three sides. Actually, let me keep the initial checks for the obvious cases (only ours changed, only theirs changed) and fall through to field-level merge for the complex cases.\\n\\nOK let me just write the code now. I've thought about this enough.\\n\\nLet me also handle the case where `base` doc doesn't exist (newly created). In the field-level merge, if `base` is `undefined`, I should treat it differently.\\n\\nActually, I think the cleanest approach is:\\n- For docs in base: field-level merge if either side diverged\\n- For docs not in base (new): handle separately\\n\\nFor the field-level merge, I need to handle the case where `base` is an object and `ours`/`theirs` could be anything.\\n\\nAlright, let me just write it all now.\\n\\nI also need to make sure that `getHistory` works on deleted documents. So I need to track documents even after deletion. Let me adjust: a document is soft-deleted (marked `deleted: true`), but still exists in the branch's doc map so we can access its history.\\n\\nSo `getHistory(id)` works on the doc even if deleted. And `get(id)` throws if deleted.\\n\\nFor the fork snapshot, I need to snapshot at branch creation time. For documents that were already deleted at that point... I think we should still include them in the snapshot? Actually no, we only snapshot the current state. If they're deleted, they effectively don't exist. But we need to know they were deleted for merge purposes.\\n\\nHmm, this is tricky. Let me simplify: the branch snapshot only captures non-deleted documents. If a doc was created and then deleted before the branch was created, it's not in the snapshot. If a doc was deleted on one branch after branching, and modified on the other, that's a conflict. The base for that doc would be... undefined (since it's not in the snapshot).\\n\\nWait, that's not right either. If I create doc X on main, then delete it from main, then branch from main, X is gone. The branch starts without X. If I then create X on the branch and modify something on main... that's a conflict (both sides created/modified from nothing).\\n\\nLet me think about what the snapshot should contain. The spec says:\\n- `createBranch(name, fromBranch?)` creates a new branch from the current branch's current state.\\n- The common ancestor state is \\\"the point where the branches diverged.\\\"\\n- For three-way merge: find the common ancestor state, then compare both branches against that base.\\n\\nSo the snapshot is just the state at the time of branching. Deleted documents don't exist. Documents that exist are snapshotted.\\n\\nBut for the merge, if base doesn't have a doc, and both sides have it, I need to check if they created it differently → conflict. If only one side has it → take it. This works.\\n\\nIf base has a doc, and ours deleted it but theirs modified it → conflict (at the root path). This also works with my logic.\\n\\nLet me code this up now, for real this time. I'll structure it as:\\n\\n```\\nsrc/\\n  index.ts    - exports DocumentStore\\n  types.ts    - interfaces\\n  merger.ts   - three-way merge logic\\n  store.ts    - DocumentStore implementation\\n```\\n\\nWait, the spec says \\\"Export a `DocumentStore` class (or factory function) from `src/index.ts`\\\". So `src/index.ts` is the entry point that exports the class. I can implement it in other files and re-export.\\n\\nLet me now write the actual implementation.\\n\\nOne more consideration: when `merge` detects no conflicts and auto-applies, we create new versions for the merged documents. Yes, the spec says the merge is \\\"auto-applied to the current branch.\\\" So we need to update the current branch's documents with the merged content and create version entries.\\n\\nLet me also think about what `getHistory` shows after a merge. The merged version becomes a new version on the current branch. So records from before the merge (on the current branch) are still in the history, and the merged version is added.\\n\\nAlright, let me write the code now.\\n\\nOne key detail on the fork snapshot: when creating branch B from branch A, I snapshot A's state at that moment. This snapshot is the common ancestor for merges between A and B. I need to store this snapshot.\\n\\nFor the snapshot, I need to only include non-deleted documents.\\n\\nLet me also think about a tricky edge case: what if we merge feature into main, then continue working on both, then merge again? The base for the second merge should still be the original fork point. This is correct because the snapshot doesn't change unless we create a new branch.\\n\\nBut actually, after the first merge, main now includes feature's changes. If we continue working on feature and merge again, we want to only merge changes since the last merge. Git handles this with merge commits, but in our simple model, we'd use the same base, which would re-apply changes that are already in main.\\n\\nHmm, this is a real problem. If we merge feature into main, then try to merge feature into main again:\\n- Base: snapshot at fork time (call it S)\\n- Ours: main (which now has feature's changes from the last merge + maybe more)\\n- Theirs: feature (which has its original changes + maybe more)\\n\\nThree-way merge: if feature's doc X was changed to X' in both branches (feature → X', main via merge → X'), and neither made additional changes, then S → X' on both sides = same value = no conflict. So they'd be merged cleanly. OK, this actually works correctly in most cases!\\n\\nBut what if after the merge, main was modified to X'' and feature was modified to X'''? Then:\\n- Base: S\\n- Ours: X''\\n- Theirs: X'''\\n- Three-way merge: S vs X'' vs X'''\\n\\nThis would compute the diff from S to X'' and from S to X'''. If the changes touch different fields, it merges cleanly. If they touch the same field differently, conflict. This is actually correct behavior! It's exactly how three-way merge works.\\n\\nSo the simple fork-point base is correct for basic usage. You don't need to update the base after merges.\\n\\nGreat, let me code it up.\\n\\nNow for the `threeWayMergeObject` function, I need to handle the case where base, ours, or theirs could be anything (object, array, primitive, null). The key insight is: arrays are leaf values (we don't deep-merge array elements), and objects are recursively merged.\\n\\nBase case for leaf values: if any of (base, ours, theirs) is not a plain object (defined as `typeof x === 'object' && x !== null && !Array.isArray(x)`), treat as leaf.\\n\\nHere's my refined merge function:\\n\\n```typescript\\nfunction threeWayMerge(\\n  base: any, ours: any, theirs: any, docId: string, path: string\\n): { merged: any, conflicts: Conflict[] } {\\n  // All three are leaf values or non-plain objects → direct comparison\\n  if (!isPlainObject(base) || !isPlainObject(ours) || !isPlainObject(theirs)) {\\n    return mergeLeaf(base, ours, theirs, docId, path);\\n  }\\n  \\n  // All three are plain objects → recursive merge\\n  const allKeys = new Set([...Object.keys(base), ...Object.keys(ours), ...Object.keys(theirs)]);\\n  const merged: Record<string, any> = {};\\n  const allConflicts: Conflict[] = [];\\n  \\n  for (const key of allKeys) {\\n    const childPath = path ? `${path}.${key}` : key;\\n    const inBase = key in base;\\n    const inOurs = key in ours;\\n    const inTheirs = key in theirs;\\n    \\n    if (inBase && inOurs && inTheirs) {\\n      const result = threeWayMerge(base[key], ours[key], theirs[key], docId, childPath);\\n      merged[key] = result.merged;\\n      allConflicts.push(...result.conflicts);\\n    } else if (inBase && inOurs && !inTheirs) {\\n      // Theirs removed the key\\n      if (deepEqual(base[key], ours[key])) {\\n        // Ours unchanged → accept removal (don't include in merged)\\n      } else {\\n        // Ours changed → conflict\\n        allConflicts.push({ documentId: docId, path: childPath, base: base[key], ours: ours[key], theirs: undefined });\\n        merged[key] = ours[key]; // ours wins for display\\n      }\\n    } else if (inBase && !inOurs && inTheirs) {\\n      // Ours removed the key\\n      if (deepEqual(base[key], theirs[key])) {\\n        // Theirs unchanged → accept removal (don't include in merged)\\n      } else {\\n        // Theirs changed → conflict\\n        allConflicts.push({ documentId: docId, path: childPath, base: base[key], ours: undefined, theirs: theirs[key] });\\n        // ours wins → keep removed (don't include in merged)\\n      }\\n    } else if (!inBase && inOurs && inTheirs) {\\n      // Both sides added the same key\\n      if (deepEqual(ours[key], theirs[key])) {\\n        merged[key] = ours[key];\\n      } else {\\n        allConflicts.push({ documentId: docId, path: childPath, base: undefined, ours: ours[key], theirs: theirs[key] });\\n        merged[key] = ours[key]; // ours wins\\n      }\\n    } else if (!inBase && inOurs) {\\n      // Only ours added\\n      merged[key] = ours[key];\\n    } else if (!inBase && inTheirs) {\\n      // Only theirs added\\n      merged[key] = theirs[key];\\n    }\\n    // key not in any of them → shouldn't happen since we union the keys\\n  }\\n  \\n  // Handle case where ours or theirs is an empty object but base has keys (all removed)\\n  // The Set union above handles this: base keys not in ours → the `inBase && !inOurs && inTheirs` case\\n  // Similarly for base keys not in theirs\\n  \\n  // Hmm, but what if a key is in base but not in ours AND not in theirs?\\n  // Both removed the same key → no conflict, don't include in merged\\n  // Our current logic doesn't handle this case. Let me fix.\\n  \\n  // Actually, the Set union only includes keys present in at least one.\\n  // If a key is in base, it's in the set. If it's not in ours AND not in theirs,\\n  // we skip it (both removed), which is correct.\\n  // But wait, that's not handled by any case. Let me add it.\\n  \\n  // I need to also handle: inBase && !inOurs && !inTheirs\\n  // Both removed the key → don't include (correct for merge)\\n  // This is a no-op skip, which is what my current code does implicitly.\\n  \\n  // Actually, let me double check the cases:\\n  // For key in base:\\n  //   if inOurs && inTheirs: merge\\n  //   if inOurs && !inTheirs: theirs removed\\n  //   if !inOurs && inTheirs: ours removed\\n  //   if !inOurs && !inTheirs: both removed → skip ✓ (implicitly handled)\\n  \\n  // For key not in base:\\n  //   if inOurs && inTheirs: both added\\n  //   if inOurs && !inTheirs: only ours added ✓\\n  //   if !inOurs && inTheirs: only theirs added ✓\\n  //   if !inOurs && !inTheirs: not in any → can't happen\\n  \\n  // OK, the logic handles all cases implicitly. \\n  \\n  // But wait, there's a case I'm not handling: what if a key is in base but \\n  // is undefined in ours/theirs? JavaScript `key in obj` returns false for\\n  // undefined values that use JSON notation (since JSON.stringify drops undefined keys).\\n  // So this is fine - we treat missing keys as absent.\\n\\n  return { merged, conflicts: allConflicts };\\n}\\n\\nfunction mergeLeaf(\\n  base: any, ours: any, theirs: any, docId: string, path: string\\n): { merged: any, conflicts: Conflict[] } {\\n  if (deepEqual(ours, theirs)) {\\n    return { merged: ours, conflicts: [] };\\n  }\\n  if (deepEqual(base, ours)) {\\n    return { merged: theirs, conflicts: [] };\\n  }\\n  if (deepEqual(base, theirs)) {\\n    return { merged: ours, conflicts: [] };\\n  }\\n  return {\\n    merged: ours, // ours wins for display\\n    conflicts: [{ documentId: docId, path, base, ours, theirs }],\\n  };\\n}\\n```\\n\\nOK this is getting quite thorough. Let me now write the actual code files.\\n\\nOne more thing: for the document-level case in the store's merge function, when `baseContent` is undefined (doc didn't exist at fork time), I should handle the case differently. Here's the flow:\\n\\n```\\nif (baseContent !== undefined) {\\n  // Doc existed at fork time\\n  if (oursDeleted && theirsDeleted) continue; // both deleted\\n  if (oursDeleted && !theirsDeleted) → conflict (root)\\n  if (!oursDeleted && theirsDeleted) → conflict (root)\\n  // Both still exist → field-level merge\\n} else {\\n  // Doc didn't exist at fork time\\n  if (oursExists && theirsExists && same) → no conflict, take it\\n  if (oursExists && theirsExists && different) → conflict (root)\\n  if (oursExists && !theirsExists) → no conflict, take ours\\n  if (!oursExists && theirsExists) → no conflict, take theirs\\n  if (!oursExists && !theirsExists) → skip\\n}\\n```\\n\\nAlright, I'm going to write the full implementation now. Let me structure the code.\\n\\nOne last thought on `deepEqual`: I'll use JSON.stringify comparison for simplicity. This works for plain objects, arrays, primitives. It has the limitation of being order-sensitive for object keys, but since JSON.stringify on V8 serializes keys in insertion order, and our deepClone uses JSON.parse(JSON.stringify(...)), the order should be consistent. Actually, the safer bet is to implement a proper deep equality check:\\n\\n```typescript\\nfunction deepEqual(a: any, b: any): boolean {\\n  if (a === b) return true;\\n  if (typeof a !== typeof b) return false;\\n  if (a === null || b === null) return a === b;\\n  if (typeof a !== 'object') return a === b;\\n  if (Array.isArray(a) !== Array.isArray(b)) return false;\\n  if (Array.isArray(a)) {\\n    if (a.length !== b.length) return false;\\n    return a.every((v, i) => deepEqual(v, b[i]));\\n  }\\n  const keysA = Object.keys(a);\\n  const keysB = Object.keys(b);\\n  if (keysA.length !== keysB.length) return false;\\n  return keysA.every(k => k in b && deepEqual(a[k], b[k]));\\n}\\n```\\n\\nThis is more reliable. Let me use this.\\n\\nNow let me also think about one more edge case in merge: what if a doc was created after the fork on one branch, and the other branch also independently created a doc with the same ID but different content? That's a conflict at the document level. My logic handles this in the `else` branch (baseContent undefined).\\n\\nAnd what about the case where a doc exists in base and is deleted on one branch but the other branch also changes it? That's a conflict at the root path. I'll represent that as `path: ''`.\\n\\nOne more thing: when the merge succeeds (no conflicts), I need to update the current branch's documents. For each document in `merged`, I update the current branch's doc and create a new version. I should also handle the case where merged has fewer docs than current (some were deleted in the merge).\\n\\nActually, re-reading the spec: \\\"A merge with no changes on either side is a no-op (fast-forward).\\\" So if neither branch changed anything, nothing happens. But also, if the merge has no conflicts and no actual changes (empty merged), it's a no-op.\\n\\nLet me code it now. I'll write three files:\\n\\n1. `src/types.ts` - Type definitions\\n2. `src/merger.ts` - Merge logic (threeWayMergeObject, deepEqual, isPlainObject)\\n3. `src/store.ts` - DocumentStore implementation  \\n4. `src/index.ts` - Re-exports\\n\\nActually, I'll put everything in `src/index.ts` and `src/merger.ts` to keep it simple. Wait, the spec says \\\"You may split into multiple files if you wish.\\\" Let me use:\\n\\n- `src/index.ts` - Main entry point (re-exports DocumentStore from store.ts)\\n- `src/store.ts` - DocumentStore class\\n- `src/merger.ts` - Merge logic\\n- `src/types.ts` - Types\\n\\nLet me write them.\\n\\nActually, to keep things simpler and avoid too many files, I'll put it in:\\n- `src/types.ts` - Type definitions\\n- `src/merger.ts` - Merge utility functions\\n- `src/index.ts` - DocumentStore class + exports\\n\\nLet me write it all now. Starting with the types.\\n\\nLet me finalize the implementation plan and write the code.\\n\\nActually, wait. Let me re-read the delete behavior more carefully. `delete(id)` removes a document from the current branch. Throws if it doesn't exist. \\n\\nThe delete should